Mean completion time of the three_interactive clients: BASE 1.35 s, BASE-WAIT 1.40 s, V7 1.86 s (a 0.54->1.72, b 1.45->2.16, c 2.07->1.70). Interleaving trades mean completion of short requests for a lower tail TTFT.

Solo short TTFT, 20 back-to-back runs after 5 warm-ups, two server restarts each: BASE 0.340 / 0.3395 s median, V7 0.341 / 0.3375 s.

## Correctness and behaviour checks (behave.py, kept in the scratchpad; results)

- Greedy text of every client in every run above is byte-identical to the sequential BASE text (solo, three_interactive, long_plus_two).
- Real-model sliced prefill (1266 tokens, slices of 64 and 128) vs one pass: normalized max 0, relative RMS 0 (bit-identical logits and one decode step later); test `crates/model/tests/qwen35_slices.rs`.
- Concurrent turn 1 of two new sessions equals the sequential text. Turn 2: session A 200 (41 tokens reused, text equals fresh), session B 404 because the single resident slot holds only the last finished session. Same session ID used by two requests at once: one 200, one 404 (in use).
- Client disconnects 1.5 s into a 1317-token prefill: the next short request completes 2.64 s after the disconnect (cancel takes effect at the next slice; the old server would finish the 10 s prefill first).
- Five long requests plus two extras with 3 active: 3 x 200, 2 x 408 (waited >10 s), both extras 503.
- Session checkpoint regression (ctx 512, 3 pairs each, medians): code pair BASE 3.511 s / V7 3.499 s, chat 3.624 / 3.622 s; turn-2 TTFT 0.494 / 0.490 and 0.517 / 0.514 s; reuse-off pairs 4.743 / 4.825 and 5.025 / 5.055 s.

### Defect found and fixed during validation

With worker threads, evicting a stored session from a request thread that had not yet made a GPU call freed device memory with no current CUDA context (cudarc does not bind the context in `CudaSlice::drop`), recording CUDA_ERROR_INVALID_CONTEXT that failed the next allocation (HTTP 500). Each worker now calls `Gpu::bind_to_thread` first. Reproduced with: session request, then any request.

### Decisions

- Slice size 64, hold 10 ms and aging 2 s were chosen once and not tuned; other values are untested.
- Continuous batching not built: after interleaving, the GPU is already 95-100% busy and aggregate throughput equals the sequential server (6.81 vs 6.82 events/s), so the measured limit is single-stream decode, not scheduling.


## Verbatim BENCHMARKS.md section before the V8 consolidation (2026-10-18)

## V7 multi-request responsiveness — GO for Qwen3.5 CUDA

Gates were fixed first ([scheduling archive](docs/benchmarks/archive/06-server-scheduling.log)).
Qwen3.5-4B Q8 CUDA, `--ctx 2048`, temperature 0, a 1,317-token job (64 output
tokens) plus 47-token interactive requests, medians of 3; "wait 120 s" is the
sequential server with the queue wait raised from 10 s for a matched baseline.
Each connection runs on its own thread (up to 3, fewer if free VRAM is short;
one on every other backend); GPU passes go through a gate, one at a time,
prompts in 64-token slices, decode steps and short prompts before full slices
(10 ms hold, 2 s aging). Sliced prefill is bit-identical to one pass.

| Metric | Sequential | Wait 120 s | Scheduled |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| Long + 2 short: short TTFT p50 / p95 | rejected (408) | 11.5 / 11.9 s | **0.47 / 0.87 s** |
| Long + 2 short: long job / events/s | 12.2 s / 5.16 | 12.5 s / 6.82 | 14.1 s / 6.81 |
| 3 short: TTFT p50 / p95; mean completion | 0.94 / 1.89 s; 1.35 s | 0.97 / 1.88 s; 1.40 s | 0.69 / 1.08 s; **1.86 s** |
| Solo short TTFT / solo long total | 0.340 s / 11.87 s | | 0.339 s / 12.35 s |

Greedy text matched the sequential server in every run; sessions stay
isolated, a mid-prefill disconnect frees the GPU at the next slice, 503/408
admission is unchanged. Round-robin lowered tail TTFT but raised short-only
mean completion 33% until decode was batched (below).

